### 9. Entry Criteria

Testing can begin when:
* Requirements analysis has been completed.
* The test strategy has been approved.
* Test scenarios have been prepared.
* The test environment is available.
* The website is available for testing.

### 10. Exit Criteria

Testing can be completed when:
* Planned test scenarios have been executed.
* Critical user flows have been validated.
* Test results have been recorded.
* Critical defects have been documented.
* A test summary report has been completed.

### 11. Defect Management

Defects identified during testing shall be documented and tracked until resolution.

Each defect report shall include:
* Defect ID
* Summary
* Steps to reproduce
* Expected result
* Actual result
* Severity
* Priority
* Supporting evidence (if applicable)

### 12. Test Deliverables

The following testing documents will be produced:
* Test Scenarios
* Test Cases (F, NF, BR, S)
* Bug Reports
* Test Summary Report

### 13. Risks and Mitigation

Website functionality may change during testing. Mitigation: Update test documentation and test cases as needed.
Limited access to test accounts or test data may restrict test coverage.	 Mitigation: Perform testing using available accounts and document any limitations.
Third-party services may be unavailable or behave unexpectedly. Mitigation: Verify user-facing functionality and document any third-party limitations.
Limited access to security features: Some tests may be blocked. Mitigation: Document limitations and mark tests as Blocked.
Limited backend visibility: Server-side security cannot be fully verified. Mitigation: Document unverified controls.
Unauthorised testing risk: Intrusive tests may affect live services. Mitigation: Perform only authorised, non-invasive tests.

